Why this exists.

Work on this machine is done by agents, and the tooling still assumes a human
is the only actor. Git alone cannot answer which repository has an agent
mid-task right now, which branch that agent created, or whether the working
tree is dirty because of your edit or because an agent left it half-finished.
Reason joins the two data sources that already exist here: the Git
repositories under ~/.ai/.src, and the opencode session database. It reads
both and shows the result on one board.

Three decisions worth recording for whoever picks this up next.

The commit graph does not use 'git log --graph'. The ASCII form throws away
lane identity across merges and re-flows its lanes as the history grows, so
there is no way to colour or hover an individual edge. We take the raw commit
list in topological order and compute the columns ourselves, which is what
makes the edges individually addressable.

There are no runtime dependencies. Node's built-in node:sqlite, node:http and
fetch cover everything. The one library that would otherwise be needed -
better-sqlite3 - is a native compile, and a native compile is the one
dependency that can fail to build on a machine where nothing else will. It
also means there is no npm install to run, nothing to audit, and nothing to
break when a transitive dependency is updated.

Every write is guarded rather than merely offered. A browser-supplied string
never becomes a Git argument: each action validates its inputs against what
the repository actually contains and then builds a fixed argument array.
Actions are previewed by running the same code path with confirm: false, so
the command shown is literally the command that would run. 'git merge-tree'
detects conflicts without touching the working tree.

The AI panel has two backends. Ollama is preferred because an answer from it
never leaves the machine. The opencode service is the fallback, because it is
the agent runtime this machine already uses, so the panel reasons with the
same models the agents do. On this machine the Ollama runtime library is
missing (llama-server is absent from ~/.local/lib/ollama, so generation
fails) even though the client and both models are installed. The panel falls
back automatically and the status chip says which backend answered, rather
than pretending the local one is working.
